## Why This Is Asked

This question probes design for scalable multi-tenant secret provisioning with per-tenant quotas, isolation, auditability, and safe rollback in Vault Enterprise across regions.

## Key Concepts

- Multi-tenant namespaces
- Per-tenant quotas (leases, sessions)
- AppRole bindings per tenant
- Audit devices and regional replication
- Rollback via lease revocation and token rotation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Node.js Vault client snippet
const vault = require('node-vault')({ endpoint: 'https://vault.example.com', token: process.env.VAULT_TOKEN });
async function ensureTenant(tenantId){
  await vault.write(`sys/policy/${tenantId}-policy`, { policy: 'path tenant/${tenantId}/* { capabilities = [read, list] }' });
  await vault.write(`auth/approle/role/${tenantId}-role`, {
    token_ttl: '24h',
    token_max_ttl: '72h',
    policies: `${tenantId}-policy`
  });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you simulate and test quota exhaustion in a staging Vault cluster?
- What metrics and alerts would you implement to detect quota policy violations?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to combine tenancy isolation with policy-as-code, Sentinel, and automation in a real Vault Enterprise onboarding scenario.

## Key Concepts
- Vault namespaces and per-tenant isolation
- AppRole bindings and per-tenant policies
- DB secret engine roles with short TTLs
- Terraform provisioning and Sentinel gate checks

## Code Example
```javascript
// Terraform-like snippet (pseudo)
provider "vault" { address = "https://vault.example" }

resource "vault_namespace" "tenant" {
  path = "ns-tenant-1"
}

resource "vault_policy" "tenant1" {
  name   = "tenant-1-policy"
  policy = <<POL
path "ns-tenant-1/secret/**" {
  capabilities = ["read","list"]
}
path "db/secret/tenant-1/**" {
  capabilities = ["read","create","update"]
}
POL
}

resource "vault_approle_auth_backend_role" "tenant1" {
  backend        = "approle"
  role_name      = "tenant-1-app"
  token_ttl      = "2m"
  token_max_ttl  = "2m"
  token_policies = ["tenant-1-policy"]
}
```

```javascript
// Sentinel-like policy (conceptual)
import "http"

allowed_paths = [
  "ns-tenant-*/secret/**",
  "db/secret/tenant-*/**"
]

main = rule {
  request.path in allowed_paths and request.namespace matches namespace
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test Sentinel drift and rollback in a live cluster?
- What metrics would you monitor during onboarding to catch isolation breaches early?
